{
  "schemaVersion": 4,
  "id": "t01-021",
  "track": "track-01",
  "title": "dates times globalization formatting and regular expressions",
  "status": "source-backed-scope-complete",
  "concepts": [
    "A date, a wall-clock time, an elapsed duration, and an instant on the UTC timeline are different values. Choose `DateOnly`, `TimeOnly`, `TimeSpan`, `DateTime`, or `DateTimeOffset` according to the meaning; a numeric time-zone offset is not the same as a named time zone with daylight-saving rules.",
    "Culture controls human-facing conventions such as number, date, casing, and sorting behavior. Persist and exchange culture-independent data with explicit invariant or standardized formats, then format it for a user with an explicit culture.",
    "Formatting converts structured values to text; parsing interprets text according to a grammar and culture. Prefer exact parsing for external contracts, handle invalid input deliberately, and avoid using localized display strings as storage formats.",
    "Regular expressions are a pattern language implemented by engines with different feature and performance tradeoffs. Backtracking patterns can exhibit catastrophic runtime; use timeouts for untrusted input or the linear-time nonbacktracking engine when its supported syntax is sufficient.",
    "Time-dependent logic becomes easier to test when code receives a `TimeProvider` and uses its clock and timer APIs instead of reading global system time directly. Use explicit time-zone conversion for future or historical local times because offsets and rules can change."
  ],
